WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.320
A wallet moves 30 from account A-1001 to A-2002.

2
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.543
They live in different partitions, on two storage nodes.

3
00:00:10.733 --> 00:00:16.436
In one partition, one node decides alone, so a single write is atomic for free.

4
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:18.896
So try two separate writes.

5
00:00:18.896 --> 00:00:20.572
First, debit A-1001.

6
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:22.838
Then the credit times out.

7
00:00:22.838 --> 00:00:25.549
30 left one account and reached none.

8
00:00:25.549 --> 00:00:27.161
The total fell to 120.

9
00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:28.184
Try again.

10
00:00:28.184 --> 00:00:34.313
DynamoDB gives the transfer, T1, to a coordinator that works in two rounds.

11
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.949
Round one, prepare.

12
00:00:35.949 --> 00:00:43.421
Node 1 checks that A-1001 holds at least 30 and is free, so it says yes and holds the item for T1.

13
00:00:43.421 --> 00:00:45.251
Nothing has changed yet.

14
00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:48.278
But another transfer, T2, holds A-2002.

15
00:00:48.278 --> 00:00:53.383
When T1's prepare arrives, that node says no at once, without waiting.

16
00:00:53.567 --> 00:00:55.671
A single no cancels everything.

17
00:00:55.671 --> 00:01:01.509
The coordinator tells node 1 to forget T1, and the caller gets a transaction conflict.

18
00:01:01.509 --> 00:01:02.867
Nothing was written.

19
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:03.951
T2 finishes.

20
00:01:03.951 --> 00:01:10.144
The caller retries T1 with the same client token, an id it picked for this transfer.

21
00:01:10.144 --> 00:01:12.281
This time both nodes say yes.

22
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:16.929
The coordinator writes commit in its ledger before it changes anything.

23
00:01:16.929 --> 00:01:21.077
If it crashed now, another coordinator would finish T1 from there.

24
00:01:21.267 --> 00:01:23.201
Round two is the commit.

25
00:01:23.201 --> 00:01:27.230
Each node applies its change and lets the item go.

26
00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.969
70 and 80: the total is still 150.

27
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:33.892
If the OK is lost, the caller sends T1 once more.

28
00:01:33.892 --> 00:01:39.823
Same token, already committed, so DynamoDB just answers OK and writes nothing.

29
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.821
The price: each item is written in both rounds, twice the write capacity.

30
00:01:44.821 --> 00:01:49.379
And held items turn others away, so busy accounts see more conflicts.

31
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:59.268
When a transaction spans partitions, every node promises first, one decision is saved where a crash can't lose it, and only then does anything change.
